1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,820
This is Doing and Feeling, the long version. Episode 10, The Evidence.

2
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:12,740
In 2008, a team led by the neuroscientist Beatrice de Gelder reported a remarkable experiment and filmed it.

3
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:21,680
Their subject, known as TN, was a physician who had suffered two strokes that destroyed the primary visual cortex on both sides of his brain.

4
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:27,120
By every clinical test, he was blind. He walked with a cane and said he saw nothing at all.

5
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:31,700
The researchers set up a long corridor cluttered with boxes and a chair,

6
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:34,720
took away his cane, and asked him to walk down it.

7
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:40,140
He did, weaving carefully around every obstacle, while insisting he could not see them.

8
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:45,340
That is the split between doing and feeling from episode one in a hospital corridor.

9
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:50,420
Something in his brain was processing visual information well enough to steer his body.

10
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:53,660
Yet, as far as he could tell, none of it was seen.

11
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:58,620
Two episodes ago, we met a novel that imagined a whole species built that way.

12
00:00:58,880 --> 00:01:01,020
Here was one man for one sense.

13
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:07,020
This episode is about evidence, what the science of consciousness can actually measure, and how far to trust it.

14
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:11,400
The methods, the patience, the hidden awareness of people who seem unresponsive,

15
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:17,280
the closest thing we have to a consciousness meter, the states that switch experience off and on, and the animals.

16
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:22,900
Along the way, we'll find that some of the field's most famous results are shakier than their reputations.

17
00:01:23,660 --> 00:01:30,220
Theories wait until next episode. Today is data. The modern program began in 1990,

18
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:35,660
when Francis Crick and Christophe Koch proposed setting the philosophical puzzle aside for a

19
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:41,260
while and hunting for the neural correlates of consciousness. David Chalmers later gave

20
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:46,640
the careful definition. A neural correlate of a conscious state is a minimal system whose

21
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:52,640
activity is sufficient, under the right background conditions, for that state. The core method is

22
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:55,220
called contrastive analysis.

23
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:57,300
Hold the stimulus constant,

24
00:01:57,680 --> 00:01:59,800
arrange for it to be consciously perceived

25
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:01,940
on some trials and not on others,

26
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:04,480
and compare brain activity between the two.

27
00:02:04,740 --> 00:02:07,420
Whatever differs is a candidate correlate.

28
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:08,920
But there's a trap,

29
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:12,140
and much of the last 15 years has been about escaping it.

30
00:02:12,440 --> 00:02:15,400
Whatever differs between seen and unseen trials

31
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:18,000
includes far more than the experience itself.

32
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:20,200
It includes prerequisites,

33
00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:23,180
like attention, expectation, and arousal,

34
00:02:23,660 --> 00:02:25,800
which must be in place before you can see anything.

35
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:29,260
And it includes consequences, the act of reporting,

36
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:32,260
the memory of having seen, your confidence about it.

37
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:35,460
In 2012, Jan Aru and colleagues,

38
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:38,000
and separately Tom de Groff and colleagues,

39
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:39,920
made this argument forcefully.

40
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:43,600
A naive contrast picks up the machinery around consciousness

41
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:45,380
along with consciousness itself.

42
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:48,760
Every machine learning person knows that problem.

43
00:02:48,980 --> 00:02:52,540
If your probe cannot separate a property from its correlates,

44
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:59,140
correlates and declare victory. Exactly, and the field's response has been to invent clever

45
00:02:59,140 --> 00:03:05,800
contrasts. Here is the toolkit, because these methods are the experimental heart of the field.

46
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:11,340
Masking, flash an image for a few hundredths of a second, then replace it with a pattern.

47
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:17,600
Set the timing at threshold, and the very same flash is seen on some trials and missed on others.

48
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:19,200
Binocular rivalry.

49
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:23,700
Show each eye a different image, say a face to one and a house to the other.

50
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:29,640
Instead of a blend, your experience alternates every few seconds, while the input never changes.

51
00:03:30,620 --> 00:03:36,420
Nikos Lagathetes and his colleagues trained monkeys to report which image they were seeing during rivalry

52
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:39,300
and recorded neurons along the visual pathway.

53
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:44,600
In the earliest visual areas, only a small minority of cells followed the reported percept.

54
00:03:45,150 --> 00:03:48,040
In an intermediate area, a bit more than a third did.

55
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:53,080
In the high-level face and object areas of the temporal lobe, about 9 in 10 did.

56
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:58,640
In those single neurons, awareness seemed to be reflected late in the hierarchy, not early.

57
00:03:58,980 --> 00:04:04,920
Later brain imaging in humans, though, found percept-linked signals even in primary visual cortex.

58
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:11,300
Continuous flash suppression, introduced by Naotsu Gutsu-chiya and Koch in 2005,

59
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:14,040
is rivalry turned up to full strength.

60
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:20,220
Flash rapidly changing colorful patterns to one eye, and a static picture shown to the other eye,

61
00:04:20,420 --> 00:04:25,500
can stay invisible for many seconds, long enough to test what the brain does with things you never

62
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:32,480
see. Inattentional blindness. In Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris's famous video, people

63
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:38,140
counting basketball passes often fail to notice a person in a gorilla suit strolling through the

64
00:04:38,140 --> 00:04:44,320
scene. Then one of the most important methodological innovations of recent years, no-report paradigms.

65
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:49,600
If report contaminates the contrast, infer what someone is experiencing without asking.

66
00:04:50,020 --> 00:04:55,560
During rivalry, the eyes make involuntary tracking movements that follow whichever image is dominant.

67
00:04:56,040 --> 00:05:04,880
In 2014, Stefan Fressler and colleagues used those movements, along with pupil size, to read out the percept with no report at all.

68
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:13,180
Frontal activity linked to rivalry was reduced when report was removed, but not abolished, and some frontal regions stayed active.

69
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:20,300
also changes how people attend, so even this cleaner contrast is not perfectly clean.

70
00:05:20,500 --> 00:05:25,860
That result is ammunition for both sides of the front versus back debate next episode.

71
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:32,960
And in October 2026, Doris Tso's lab, which had already used a no-report design with single face

72
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:38,960
cells, posted a perprint based on recordings from thousands of neurons at once. Two monkeys

73
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:44,780
passively watched displays in which one face was flashed and another suppressed. In face-selective

74
00:05:44,780 --> 00:05:49,660
regions, activity flipped back and forth between representing the seen face and the suppressed one,

75
00:05:50,020 --> 00:05:56,300
each state lasting roughly 60 milliseconds. It's two animals from one lab, not yet peer-reviewed,

76
00:05:56,360 --> 00:06:02,060
and the face counted as seen was the one human viewers reported. A switching rate is not a frame

77
00:06:02,060 --> 00:06:07,580
rate for experience, but it hints that what you see may be a rapidly contested state rather than

78
00:06:09,260 --> 00:06:12,920
Now a measurement problem that sounds technical and is not.

79
00:06:13,140 --> 00:06:15,260
How do you know someone did not see something?

80
00:06:15,520 --> 00:06:17,980
The obvious answer is that they told you.

81
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:20,340
But a report depends on two things.

82
00:06:20,560 --> 00:06:24,860
How much information gets through and how willing a person is to say yes.

83
00:06:25,320 --> 00:06:29,760
Signal detection theory, borrowed from radar engineering, separates them.

84
00:06:30,140 --> 00:06:33,140
Sensitivity is how well you can tell signal from noise.

85
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:37,960
Criterion is how much evidence you demand before saying, I saw it.

86
00:06:38,960 --> 00:06:45,660
A cautious observer with a strict criterion will say nothing was there on many trials where some faint experience was present.

87
00:06:45,940 --> 00:06:54,360
This is the criterion problem, and in 2021, the philosopher Ian Phillips used it to challenge one of the fields found in cases.

88
00:06:55,100 --> 00:06:59,120
Blindsight, he argued, may not be vision without experience at all.

89
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:08,040
It may be degraded conscious vision, so faint and strange that patients, applying the standard of normal sight, sincerely report seeing nothing.

90
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:17,260
On that reading, blindsight shows how unreliable a report of nothing can be, rather than showing that sophisticated vision can run in the dark.

91
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:23,260
So the most famous evidence for doing without feeling might be feeling that's too weak to count.

92
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:28,860
That's the live question, and worth holding in mind for every patient case in this episode.

93
00:07:29,480 --> 00:07:37,000
Defenders of the classic reading point to signal detection studies suggesting that blindsight differs from faint, normal vision.

94
00:07:39,220 --> 00:07:45,460
but it has pushed the field to measure confidence and response bias far more routinely than it once did.

95
00:07:46,420 --> 00:07:48,680
With that caution in hand, the patients.

96
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:51,760
Blindsight was named by Lawrence Weisskrantz,

97
00:07:51,940 --> 00:07:56,840
whose patient DB had part of his primary visual cortex surgically removed.

98
00:07:57,380 --> 00:08:01,340
DB denied seeing anything in the affected half of his visual field,

99
00:08:01,540 --> 00:08:04,780
yet when forced to guess, he could point to objects there

100
00:08:04,780 --> 00:08:08,480
and discriminate orientation and movement far above chance.

101
00:08:08,960 --> 00:08:15,020
Weisskrantz and colleagues' key report appeared in 1974 and his monograph in 1986.

102
00:08:15,660 --> 00:08:19,120
Some blindsight patients report no experience at all,

103
00:08:19,340 --> 00:08:21,740
others a vague feeling that something is there.

104
00:08:21,960 --> 00:08:23,800
And there's effective blindsight.

105
00:08:24,180 --> 00:08:30,220
Patients respond, above chance, to the emotional expressions of faces they say they cannot see.

106
00:08:30,900 --> 00:08:33,400
Hemispatial neglect is in some ways stranger.

107
00:08:33,780 --> 00:08:36,800
After damage typically to the right parietal lobe,

108
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:44,040
patients ignore the left side of space. In 1978, Edordo Bisioc and Claudio Lutzati

109
00:08:44,040 --> 00:08:49,520
asked neglect patients in Milan to imagine standing in the cathedral square and describe

110
00:08:49,520 --> 00:08:55,580
what they saw. They described only the buildings on their imagined right. Asked to imagine standing

111
00:08:55,580 --> 00:09:00,740
at the opposite end of the square, they described the other side and omitted what they had just

112
00:09:00,740 --> 00:09:06,720
described. The neglect lived in the representation of space itself. Then there's the most famous

113
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:13,040
neglect case of all. In 1988, John Marshall and Peter Halligan reported a patient shown two

114
00:09:13,040 --> 00:09:17,880
drawings of a house, identical, except that one had flames coming from its left side.

115
00:09:18,140 --> 00:09:22,920
She said the drawings were the same. Yet when asked which house she would rather live in,

116
00:09:23,100 --> 00:09:28,540
she mostly chose the one that was not burning. It became a textbook example of unconscious

117
00:09:28,540 --> 00:09:33,800
information guiding choice. Here's what the textbooks often leave out. It was a single

118
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:41,400
patient. A 1990 follow-up by Bisioc and Maria Luiser-Rosconi got inconsistent results. And in

119
00:09:41,400 --> 00:09:48,600
2025, a pre-registered replication tested 54 stroke patients, 21 of them with neglect. The

120
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:54,640
burning house effect appeared in just one patient, who did not have neglect. Exploratory analyses

121
00:09:54,640 --> 00:10:00,560
suggested that when people did show a preference, they preferred intact drawings over merely shaded

122
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:06,620
ones, so the effect may not have been about fire at all. One patient and possibly about shading,

123
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:13,540
not fire. How many textbook cases rest on one patient? More than you'd like. Others have held

124
00:10:13,540 --> 00:10:19,920
up well, like the imagined paiza. The lesson is to weigh a famous single case by how often it has

125
00:10:19,920 --> 00:10:25,480
been reproduced, rather than by how often it has been retold. Then the split-brain patients.

126
00:10:25,860 --> 00:10:29,860
To treat severe epilepsy, some patients had the corpus callosum,

127
00:10:30,060 --> 00:10:32,980
the main bridge between the hemispheres, surgically cut.

128
00:10:33,440 --> 00:10:36,800
Roger Sperry, who shared a Nobel Prize for this work,

129
00:10:36,940 --> 00:10:41,840
and Michael Gazzaniga showed that each hemisphere could respond to information the other did not have.

130
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:44,500
In Gazzaniga's most famous demonstration,

131
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:47,400
the speaking left hemisphere saw a chicken claw,

132
00:10:47,580 --> 00:10:50,060
and the right hemisphere saw a snowy scene.

133
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:52,180
The right hand picked a picture of a chicken,

134
00:10:52,180 --> 00:10:55,940
and the left hand, controlled by the right hemisphere, picked a shovel.

135
00:10:56,420 --> 00:11:01,280
Asked why, the left hemisphere, which never saw the snow, answered without hesitation.

136
00:11:01,580 --> 00:11:05,720
The claw goes with the chicken, and you need a shovel to clean out the chicken shed.

137
00:11:06,400 --> 00:11:09,520
Gazzaniga called the system behind such answers the interpreter,

138
00:11:09,860 --> 00:11:16,300
a process that generates fluent, confident explanations for behavior whose real causes it cannot see.

139
00:11:16,580 --> 00:11:22,080
For anyone who works with language models, that's the most important result in this episode.

140
00:11:22,180 --> 00:11:31,420
An articulate explanation of a system's own behavior tells you nothing, on its own, about whether the explanation is causally accurate.

141
00:11:31,760 --> 00:11:40,840
That's the parallel drawn in Episode 6, where a model described carrying digits it never carried and will hold onto it for Episodes 12 and 13.

142
00:11:41,060 --> 00:11:43,680
And it is not confined to split brains.

143
00:11:43,680 --> 00:11:48,980
In choice blindness experiments, the next entry in Episode 8's File on Introspection,

144
00:11:49,560 --> 00:11:54,460
Petter Johansson and Lars Hall had people choose which of two faces they found more attractive,

145
00:11:54,840 --> 00:11:59,160
then secretly handed them the other photo and asked them to explain their choice.

146
00:11:59,620 --> 00:12:03,700
On most of the manipulated trials, people did not notice the swap,

147
00:12:03,860 --> 00:12:07,440
and explained why they had preferred a face they had actually rejected.

148
00:12:07,740 --> 00:12:11,020
Even the split brain itself is less settled than it looks.

149
00:12:11,460 --> 00:12:15,580
In 2017, Yer Pinto and colleagues reported two patients

150
00:12:15,580 --> 00:12:19,520
who could respond to stimuli anywhere in the visual field with either hand,

151
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:22,580
though they could not compare stimuli across the two halves,

152
00:12:22,760 --> 00:12:27,580
and suggested that perception may be split while the person remains one conscious agent.

153
00:12:27,860 --> 00:12:30,560
With two patients, that is contested.

154
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:36,580
The most consequential findings concern patients who seem completely unresponsive.

155
00:12:36,920 --> 00:12:38,380
Clinically, there's a spectrum.

156
00:12:38,860 --> 00:12:41,300
In coma, patients cannot be roused.

157
00:12:41,520 --> 00:12:45,920
In the vegetative state, now often called unresponsive wakefulness syndrome,

158
00:12:46,300 --> 00:12:50,660
they have sleep-wake cycles and open their eyes, but show no sign of awareness.

159
00:12:50,960 --> 00:12:55,780
In the minimally conscious state, signs of awareness are inconsistent but genuine.

160
00:12:56,520 --> 00:12:58,400
Bedside diagnosis is hard.

161
00:12:58,660 --> 00:13:03,060
In a well-known 2009 study using a careful standardized scale,

162
00:13:03,420 --> 00:13:08,280
18 of 44 patients diagnosed as vegetative by clinical consensus

163
00:13:08,380 --> 00:13:15,660
about 41 percent turned out to be minimally conscious. In 2006, Adrian Owen and colleagues

164
00:13:15,660 --> 00:13:22,400
published a short paper in Science that changed the field. A 23-year-old woman, five months after

165
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:29,620
a traffic accident, met every criterion for the vegetative state. In an FMRI scanner, she was asked

166
00:13:29,620 --> 00:13:35,120
to imagine playing tennis and then to imagine walking through the rooms of her house. Her brain

167
00:13:35,120 --> 00:13:40,620
activity matched that of healthy volunteers, motor planning areas for tennis, spatial navigation

168
00:13:40,620 --> 00:13:46,600
areas for the house. She was following instructions. It was a single patient, and critics wondered

169
00:13:46,600 --> 00:13:52,320
whether the words alone triggered automatic responses. But in 2010, a larger study found

170
00:13:52,320 --> 00:13:58,020
that 5 of 54 patients could modulate their brain activity this way, and one could answer yes or no

171
00:13:58,020 --> 00:14:05,100
questions, imagining tennis for yes and the house for no. Then, in 2024, a study in the New England

172
00:14:05,120 --> 00:14:12,320
Journal of Medicine led by Yelena Bodian tested 353 patients at six centers in Europe and North

173
00:14:12,320 --> 00:14:20,080
America. Of the 241 who could not follow commands at the bedside, 60, a quarter, showed covert

174
00:14:20,080 --> 00:14:26,660
responses on brain imaging or E. E.g., the field calls this cognitive motor dissociation,

175
00:14:26,820 --> 00:14:33,340
and among patients who could follow commands at the bedside, only about 38% showed responses on

176
00:14:35,120 --> 00:14:40,420
miss many people who are plainly aware, so a negative scan cannot rule awareness out.

177
00:14:40,660 --> 00:14:46,780
A quarter in this group, but following commands in a scanner is still access, in block sense.

178
00:14:47,060 --> 00:14:52,960
Does it tell us anything about experience? In a person, access is the best evidence of

179
00:14:52,960 --> 00:14:59,000
experience we ever get, and we grant it to each other on far less. So it raises urgent questions

180
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:03,240
about pain, communication, and decisions to withdraw care.

181
00:15:03,700 --> 00:15:06,280
And it's the clearest demonstration that behavior

182
00:15:06,280 --> 00:15:08,520
can radically underestimate consciousness.

183
00:15:09,460 --> 00:15:11,820
Can we measure consciousness directly

184
00:15:11,820 --> 00:15:14,120
without asking the person to do anything?

185
00:15:14,420 --> 00:15:18,360
The closest thing we have is the Perturbational Complexity Index,

186
00:15:18,620 --> 00:15:22,840
developed by Marcello Massimini, Giulio Tononi, and colleagues,

187
00:15:23,100 --> 00:15:24,880
and first published in 2013.

188
00:15:25,300 --> 00:15:27,460
It's nicknamed Zap and Zip.

189
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:36,160
with a pulse of transcranial magnetic stimulation and record the brain's echo with EEG. Then you

190
00:15:36,160 --> 00:15:41,660
zip it. You compress the spatial and temporal pattern of the response, using the same kind of

191
00:15:41,660 --> 00:15:46,700
algorithm that compresses computer files. A conscious brain answers a perturbation in a way

192
00:15:46,700 --> 00:15:51,840
that is both integrated, spreading widely, and differentiated, producing a complex,

193
00:15:52,040 --> 00:15:57,920
hard-to-compress pattern. An unconscious brain responds either locally, like a stone dropped in

194
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:04,580
a big stereotyped wave, like a bell ringing. Either way, the response compresses easily.

195
00:16:04,840 --> 00:16:10,460
In the first study, values in dreamless sleep and under anesthesia fell roughly between 0.1

196
00:16:10,460 --> 00:16:17,060
and 0.3, and wakefulness ran from about 0.4 to 0.7. Vegetative patients were low,

197
00:16:17,440 --> 00:16:22,880
minimally conscious patients intermediate and locked-in patients, fully conscious but paralyzed,

198
00:16:22,880 --> 00:16:29,880
in the waking range. In 2016, Sylvia Cacerado and colleagues calibrated a cutoff of 0.31

199
00:16:29,880 --> 00:16:36,180
that separated conscious from unconscious conditions in a benchmark group of 150 people

200
00:16:36,180 --> 00:16:43,200
without a single error. Applied to 43 vegetative patients, it flagged 9 with high complexity,

201
00:16:43,440 --> 00:16:49,160
and 6 of those 9 later recovered signs of awareness. The most striking calibration case

202
00:16:49,160 --> 00:16:55,600
is ketamine. At anesthetic doses, it leaves people completely unresponsive. But they often

203
00:16:55,600 --> 00:17:00,800
report vivid dreams afterward, and under ketamine, the complexity index stayed high,

204
00:17:01,260 --> 00:17:05,320
matching what people later reported rather than how they looked from the outside.

205
00:17:05,760 --> 00:17:11,020
Notice where the measure's credibility comes from, though. It's calibrated against report.

206
00:17:11,340 --> 00:17:16,640
Right. Every consciousness meter is ultimately validated against people who can tell us,

207
00:17:16,640 --> 00:17:22,480
afterward whether they experienced anything. That's fine for humans. It becomes the deep

208
00:17:22,480 --> 00:17:28,860
problem for machines in episode 12. Some of the most powerful evidence comes from switching

209
00:17:28,860 --> 00:17:34,620
consciousness off and back on. General anesthesia is the only routine procedure that reliably

210
00:17:34,620 --> 00:17:40,280
interrupts experience and then returns it. But unresponsive is not the same as unconscious.

211
00:17:40,880 --> 00:17:46,480
There's an old, simple trick called the isolated forearm technique. Before the muscle relax

212
00:17:46,640 --> 00:17:53,320
is given, a tourniquet is inflated on one arm, so that hand can still move. In a 2017 study across

213
00:17:53,320 --> 00:18:00,280
six hospitals, 260 patients were asked, after intubation, to squeeze with that hand. Twelve

214
00:18:00,280 --> 00:18:05,920
of them, about four and a half percent, responded to the command. None of those questioned afterward

215
00:18:05,920 --> 00:18:11,620
remembered anything. So for some patients, under some regimes, there is connected awareness without

216
00:18:11,620 --> 00:18:17,840
memory, and the absence of a memory is not proof that nothing was experienced. Anesthetics act

217
00:18:17,840 --> 00:18:23,300
through quite different molecular mechanisms, yet the ones that abolish experience seem to share a

218
00:18:23,300 --> 00:18:29,980
breakdown of integrated communication across the brain. In September 2026, a study led by Andrea

219
00:18:29,980 --> 00:18:37,160
Luppy reported a common signature of anesthesia in six species—humans, macaques, marmosets, mice,

220
00:18:37,160 --> 00:18:43,500
zebrafish, and nematode worms. The drugs and recording methods differed. But in every species,

221
00:18:43,940 --> 00:18:49,580
activity in different parts of the nervous system became less coordinated, and its past predicted

222
00:18:49,580 --> 00:18:56,500
its future less well. Deep structures matter too. In 2020, Michelle Redinbaugh and colleagues found

223
00:18:56,500 --> 00:19:02,040
that stimulating a small region of the central thalamus could wake anesthetized macaques while

224
00:19:02,040 --> 00:19:08,400
the anesthetic was still flowing. And in 2025, deep electrodes in five human patients showed

225
00:19:08,400 --> 00:19:13,820
certain thalamic nuclei becoming active before the prefrontal cortex on trials where a stimulus

226
00:19:13,820 --> 00:19:19,380
was consciously seen, as if the thalamus were a gate. Sleep makes the same point from another

227
00:19:19,380 --> 00:19:25,380
angle. You're not unconscious all night. You dream, and not only in REM sleep. In 2017,

228
00:19:25,920 --> 00:19:30,220
Francesca Sicklery, Tononi, and colleagues woke people repeatedly through the night

229
00:19:32,860 --> 00:19:39,380
anything. Dreaming, in both REM and non-REM sleep, went with less slow-wave activity and more fast

230
00:19:39,380 --> 00:19:45,360
activity in a posterior region of the cortex. In a follow-up with seven sleepers, that signal alone

231
00:19:45,360 --> 00:19:51,860
predicted in real time, during non-REM sleep, whether they would report a dream, with about 87%

232
00:19:51,860 --> 00:19:59,360
accuracy. We'll meet that posterior hot zone again next episode. And in 2021, four labs reported

233
00:19:59,360 --> 00:20:05,140
two-way communication with lucid dreamers who solved simple arithmetic problems during REM sleep

234
00:20:05,140 --> 00:20:10,580
and signaled their answers with eye movements. Psychedelics push in yet another direction.

235
00:20:11,080 --> 00:20:17,100
Robin Carhart-Harris's entropic brain hypothesis and the model he developed with Carl Friston

236
00:20:17,100 --> 00:20:22,420
called Rebus proposed that psychedelics relax the brain's high-level prior beliefs,

237
00:20:22,760 --> 00:20:27,880
letting bottom-up signals flow more freely. That connects directly to the predictive brain of

238
00:20:29,360 --> 00:20:34,240
fits the finding that the diversity of brain signals, a cousin of ZAP and ZIP's compression

239
00:20:34,240 --> 00:20:41,900
measure, rises under psilocybin, LSD, and ketamine. In 2024, a study in Nature by Joshua

240
00:20:41,900 --> 00:20:47,760
Segel and colleagues scanned people repeatedly before, during, and after a dose of psilocybin.

241
00:20:47,900 --> 00:20:53,220
The drug desynchronized brain networks more than three times as much as a stimulant control,

242
00:20:53,600 --> 00:20:58,860
most strongly in the default mode network, the system most associated with self-referential

243
00:20:59,360 --> 00:21:04,040
And reduced coupling between the hippocampus and that network persisted for weeks.

244
00:21:04,960 --> 00:21:10,260
Then there's a source of evidence that is very old and newly scientific, meditation.

245
00:21:10,940 --> 00:21:15,660
Contemplative traditions have been mapping states of mind for more than 2,000 years.

246
00:21:16,120 --> 00:21:21,360
The Mandukya Upanishad distinguishes waking, dreaming, and dreamless sleep

247
00:21:21,360 --> 00:21:24,420
from a fourth state it describes as pure awareness.

248
00:21:24,760 --> 00:21:28,660
Buddhist traditions describe meditative absorptions called jhanas

249
00:21:29,360 --> 00:21:35,600
state called Nirodha Samapati, the attainment of cessation. These are first-person claims,

250
00:21:35,840 --> 00:21:41,280
made by people who have trained their attention for decades. On their own, they're not data in

251
00:21:41,280 --> 00:21:47,380
the scientist's sense, but they generate hypotheses that can now be tested. The philosopher Thomas

252
00:21:47,380 --> 00:21:54,660
Metzinger's 2024 book, The Elephant and the Blind, analyzes more than 500 reports from meditators of

253
00:21:54,660 --> 00:22:00,140
what he calls minimal phenomenal experience. Awareness with little or no content, and no

254
00:22:00,140 --> 00:22:06,200
sense of self or time. If that state exists, it's a precious test case for any theory that ties

255
00:22:06,200 --> 00:22:13,020
consciousness to self-modeling or to rich content. And in 2023, Ruben Lachonin, Matthew Sackett,

256
00:22:13,320 --> 00:22:17,280
Helene Slogter, and colleagues set out a research program on meditative cessations.

257
00:22:17,740 --> 00:22:22,380
Brief, apparently complete gaps in consciousness that some advanced practitioners say they can

258
00:22:22,380 --> 00:22:28,040
induce at will, and much longer ones in the traditional accounts. Early EEG work has studied

259
00:22:28,040 --> 00:22:34,040
a single advanced meditator intensively, across repeated cessation events. All of this is early,

260
00:22:34,280 --> 00:22:41,200
small, and dependent on trained reports. Trained reports are still reports. Why trust a meditator

261
00:22:41,200 --> 00:22:47,380
more than the shoppers of Episode 8? On reports alone, you should not, and every caveat about

262
00:22:47,380 --> 00:22:53,140
introspection from episode 8 applies. What training buys is consistency and the chance

263
00:22:53,140 --> 00:22:58,600
to pair reports with measurements. That's Varela's neurophenomenology from last episode

264
00:22:58,600 --> 00:23:04,220
finally getting data. And if consciousness can be switched off by training as well as by drugs,

265
00:23:04,480 --> 00:23:10,980
that's an experimental handle no anesthesiologist has. And any complete theory has to account for

266
00:23:10,980 --> 00:23:16,620
the extremes. The cessations, the dreams under Kerhameen, the awareness without content.

267
00:23:17,620 --> 00:23:23,680
The free will experiments deserve care because they're so often summarized wrongly. In 1983,

268
00:23:24,320 --> 00:23:28,680
Benjamin Libet asked people to flex their wrist whenever they felt like it, and to note,

269
00:23:28,900 --> 00:23:34,840
using a fast-moving clock when they first felt the urge. A slow buildup of electrical activity,

270
00:23:35,060 --> 00:23:39,640
the readiness potential, began a few hundred milliseconds before the reported moment of

271
00:23:40,980 --> 00:23:44,040
The headline wrote itself, your brain decides before you do.

272
00:23:44,500 --> 00:23:46,480
Three later findings complicate it.

273
00:23:46,840 --> 00:23:59,380
In 2012, Aaron Scherger, Jacobo Sit, and Stanislas DeHaan showed that the readiness potential can be explained as random neural noise drifting toward a threshold, with the movement launched when it crosses.

274
00:24:00,080 --> 00:24:04,080
Averaged backward from the moment of movement, that noise looks like a ramp.

275
00:24:04,240 --> 00:24:07,940
On this reading, the early signal is not a hidden decision at all.

276
00:24:08,460 --> 00:24:14,780
In 2008, Chun Siang Soon, John Dylan Haynes, and colleagues decoded which of two buttons

277
00:24:14,780 --> 00:24:20,900
someone would press from FMRI activity several seconds in advance, but with accuracy only

278
00:24:20,900 --> 00:24:23,520
around 60%, where chance is 50.

279
00:24:23,720 --> 00:24:29,460
And in 2019, Yuri Ma Os and colleagues had people decide which of two charities would

280
00:24:29,460 --> 00:24:31,920
receive a real $1,000 donation.

281
00:24:32,580 --> 00:24:37,700
The readiness potential was present when the choice was arbitrary, and absent or much

282
00:24:53,900 --> 00:24:57,560
Here's a question that connects back to the first part of the series.

283
00:24:57,980 --> 00:25:03,280
If machines can do so much without consciousness, how much can humans do without it?

284
00:25:03,560 --> 00:25:05,180
Less than the hype suggested.

285
00:25:05,580 --> 00:25:08,500
The famous 1957 claimed that flashing the words

286
00:25:08,500 --> 00:25:11,280
eat popcorn in a cinema-boosted sales was a hoax.

287
00:25:11,540 --> 00:25:14,780
In 1962, its author admitted it had been a gimmick,

288
00:25:14,940 --> 00:25:16,660
with too little data to mean anything.

289
00:25:17,280 --> 00:25:18,560
Unconscious thought theory,

290
00:25:18,820 --> 00:25:21,040
which claimed that complex decisions go better

291
00:25:21,040 --> 00:25:23,800
when you're distracted, has largely failed to replicate.

292
00:25:24,400 --> 00:25:27,400
Masked priming is real, but in careful studies,

293
00:25:27,420 --> 00:25:29,420
its effects are small and short-lived.

294
00:25:29,600 --> 00:25:31,900
The picture that has survived is roughly this.

295
00:25:32,340 --> 00:25:34,820
Unconscious processing in humans is fast,

296
00:25:35,180 --> 00:25:41,080
parallel, and specialized, and can extract some meaning from words. But flexible, multi-step

297
00:25:41,080 --> 00:25:47,080
reasoning and acting on novel combinations seem to depend on conscious access. Holding information

298
00:25:47,080 --> 00:25:53,140
over time mostly does too, though some studies find faint traces of unseen items lasting a few

299
00:25:53,140 --> 00:26:00,320
seconds. Which is interesting, because in machines that coupling apparently does not hold. Language

300
00:26:00,320 --> 00:26:04,900
models do multi-step reasoning, and most researchers doubt they're conscious.

301
00:26:05,380 --> 00:26:10,900
That's one of the deepest puzzles where our two topics meet. Either human brains couple flexible

302
00:26:10,900 --> 00:26:15,980
reasoning with consciousness for contingent architectural reasons, in which case machines

303
00:26:15,980 --> 00:26:21,780
show the coupling is optional. Or machines that reason flexibly have something functionally like

304
00:26:21,780 --> 00:26:28,580
conscious access, whether or not anything is experienced. Episodes 12 and 13 take both readings

305
00:26:30,620 --> 00:26:31,900
Finally, animals.

306
00:26:32,260 --> 00:26:37,000
In 2020, Andreas Nieder and colleagues trained two carrion crows

307
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:39,580
to report whether they had seen a faint stimulus.

308
00:26:40,260 --> 00:26:43,280
Neurons in a high-level region of the crow forebrain

309
00:26:43,280 --> 00:26:45,620
tracked stimulus intensity at first,

310
00:26:45,760 --> 00:26:47,760
but during the delay before the response,

311
00:26:48,020 --> 00:26:50,200
they tracked what the crow went on to report,

312
00:26:50,460 --> 00:26:52,100
including its false alarms.

313
00:26:52,460 --> 00:26:55,920
That's a neural correlate of subjective report in a bird,

314
00:26:56,060 --> 00:26:58,840
whose brain, as we saw in episode two,

315
00:27:00,320 --> 00:27:07,260
like circuits by a separate evolutionary route. In April 2024, a group of scientists and philosophers

316
00:27:07,260 --> 00:27:12,880
launched the New York Declaration on Animal Consciousness. It states that there is strong

317
00:27:12,880 --> 00:27:18,520
scientific support for attributing conscious experience to mammals and birds. It states that

318
00:27:18,520 --> 00:27:23,560
there is at least a realistic possibility of it in all vertebrates, and in many invertebrates,

319
00:27:23,840 --> 00:27:29,580
including at least cephalopods, decapod crustaceans, and insects. And it says that

320
00:27:30,320 --> 00:27:34,100
to ignore that possibility in decisions affecting those animals.

321
00:27:34,520 --> 00:27:38,980
About 40 researchers signed at the launch, and hundreds more have signed since.

322
00:27:39,180 --> 00:27:44,820
The method behind statements like that is what the philosopher Jonathan Birch calls a theory-light approach.

323
00:27:45,180 --> 00:27:51,080
Rather than commit to one theory, look for behavioral markers associated with conscious experience in humans.

324
00:27:51,500 --> 00:27:55,120
Do animals trade off avoiding pain against getting something they want?

325
00:27:55,340 --> 00:27:59,120
Do they tend their wounds or learn to avoid places where they were hurt?

326
00:28:00,320 --> 00:28:04,440
given small shocks are more willing to abandon a poor shell than a good one.

327
00:28:04,640 --> 00:28:10,360
In 2021, Robin Crook gave octopuses a painful injection. They tended the spot,

328
00:28:10,680 --> 00:28:15,320
later avoided the chamber where it happened, and preferred one where a local anesthetic had

329
00:28:15,320 --> 00:28:21,580
brought relief. The octopus from episode two, it seems, can hurt, and the ball-rolling bumblebees

330
00:28:21,580 --> 00:28:28,220
from episode two met the behavioral criteria for play. Episode 14 returns to how evidence

331
00:28:28,220 --> 00:28:32,740
of this kind changed the law in Britain. And none of that transfers to AI.

332
00:28:33,440 --> 00:28:39,320
That's the point to end on. Every marker in this episode was calibrated in creatures that share

333
00:28:39,320 --> 00:28:45,900
our evolutionary history and our biology. In animals, behavior and biology come bundled,

334
00:28:45,940 --> 00:28:51,100
so a behavioral marker is evidence about the machinery underneath. A language model can

335
00:28:51,100 --> 00:28:55,980
produce any behavioral marker you can name, trade-off talk and pain talk included,

336
00:28:55,980 --> 00:28:59,880
because it learned from text written by creatures that have the machinery.

337
00:29:00,240 --> 00:29:03,580
For machines, theory-like approaches break down

338
00:29:03,580 --> 00:29:05,480
and were forced back onto theory,

339
00:29:06,090 --> 00:29:09,000
which is why the next episode puts the theories on trial.

